    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d358">

                <pb facs="0554" n="478" sameAs="frus:frus1935v02/pg478"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">462.11L5232/523: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Dodd</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-02-26T17:00:00+01:00">February 26, 1935—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 26—2:20 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>32. Department’s 15, February 21, 6 p.m. Foreign Office states that sabotage
                    evidence, hitherto delayed owing to need for careful analysis by reason of
                    inaccuracy of witnesses, is being hurried and promises <pb facs="0555" n="479"
                        xml:id="pg479"/> that instructions together with the first section of the
                    evidence will be forwarded to the German Embassy through which channel only it
                    prefers to act by middle of next week,<anchor n="32" xml:id="d358-f1"/> the
                    final section following soon after.</p>
                <p>Embassy will be informed immediately instructions referred to are sent. I will
                    cable Department. Mail report follows.<anchor n="33" xml:id="d358-f2"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dodd</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d358-f1" n="32" xml:id="d358-f1-content">
                <p>By telegram No. 41, March 6, 1935, 4 p.m., the Ambassador in Germany informed the
                    Department that the instructions had been sent to the German Embassy.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d358-f2" n="33" xml:id="d358-f2-content">
                <p>Despatch No. 1813, February 28, 1935, not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
